  "title": "Erdős Problem #776",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $r\\geq 2$ and $A_1,\\ldots,A_m\\subseteq \\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ be such that $A_i\not\\subseteq A_j$ for all $i\neq j$ and for any $t$ if there exists some $i$ with $\\lvert A_i\\rvert=t$ then there must exist at least $r$ sets of that size.\nHow large must $n$ be (as a function of $r$) to ensure that there is such a family which achieves $n-3$ distinct sizes of sets?",
